  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 233 AM MDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nOn Sunday and into Tuesday morning, a low pressure over the Four\nCorners region will provide southwesterly flow aloft is still\npersistent over the forecast area until Monday evening. During\nthis time, a high-pressure system over the Gulf will start\nbringing in low- to mid-level moisture. With the vorticity and\nmoisture advection, this could lead to long lasting showers over\nthe area until Monday evening. Showers on Sunday and Monday are\nforecast to be widespread across the County Warning Area (CWA).\nThere is high confidence (70%) that rain totals could exceed a\ntenth (0.10) of an inch over the area. Precipitation will\ncontinue from the short term period and persist until early\nTuesday morning.\n\nDuring this event, northerly cold air advection will\nconsistently cool down temperatures. High temperatures for\nSunday are forecast to be in the 50s to low 60s and then cooling\n5 to 7 degrees. Low temperatures for Monday are forecast to be\nfrom the mid 40s in the southeast and mid 30s in the northwest\nof the CWA and then cool down an additional 5 to 8 degrees\nTuesday. With this, there is potential for a brief period of \nnear freezing temperatures before the precipitation moves out, \nintroducing a brief rain/snow mix. The most likely area to see \nthis mix is northwest of a line from Vona, Colorado to Swanson \nLake around sunrise Tuesday morning. No snow accumulation is \nexpected at this point.\n\nOn Tuesday, the pattern starts to become uncertain as the trough\nweakens and slides north over the Kansas-Nebraska state line.\nOnce it moves through, a ridge axis will slide into the area.\nWhile it is favored for the ridge to pass through on Tuesday,\nthere are some timing issues within guidance that could either\nspeed up or delay the ridge. If the ridge speeds up, this could\nlead to showers moving out of the area sooner than expected and\nhelp temperatures increase. If the ridge slows down, we then\ncould see showers midday tuesday with slightly cooler\ntemperatures. After this, the ridge will lead into a warming\ntrend lasting into Wednesday with highs in the mid 60s.",
